<Project>

  <!-- 🚨 DELIBERATELY EMPTY — and it must stay that way.

       `VerifyXunitRunnerConfigCopied` used to live here. It now lives in
       test/Directory.Build.props, in the SAME file as the four Content includes it validates,
       and moving it back would re-open the hole it was moved to close.

       MSBuild auto-imports Directory.Build.props and Directory.Build.targets independently, so a
       consumer can take one and not the other — and every satellite that consumes the plumbing
       does exactly that. MeshWeaver.Plugins (78 *.Test projects) and MeshWeaver.SocialMedia (1)
       import `$(MeshWeaverRoot)/test/Directory.Build.props` explicitly from their own
       src/Directory.Build.props, and NEITHER imports this file. Measured 2026-09-01: ZERO of the
       five satellite checkouts (.Plugins, .Education, .Reinsurance, .SocialMedia, .Manufacturing)
       reference `test/Directory.Build.targets` at all. They inherited the plumbing without the
       assertion that it worked — 79 test projects, against core's ~15 that had the guard.

       The rule AGENTS.md states, and the reason this file is now empty: a gate must never be
       separable from the input it validates — a gate that never runs is indistinguishable from a
       gate that passed. Anything guarding test/Directory.Build.props belongs IN
       test/Directory.Build.props. See DirectoryBuildGuardTravelsWithItsSubjectTest, which fails if
       the target reappears here.

       The file itself is kept (rather than deleted) so the auto-import surface of the test tree is
       unchanged: MSBuild stops its upward Directory.Build.targets search at the first file it
       finds, and there is no root-level Directory.Build.targets in this repository, so keeping an
       empty one preserves today's behaviour exactly. -->

</Project>
